{"key":"soil","updated":"2026-10-21","label":"Soil moisture","title":"Root-zone soil moisture compared with normal","subtitle":"Sep 2026, against Sep in 11 earlier years","source":"NASA SMAP L4 (SPL4SMGP v008)","demo":false,"build":1,"categories":[{"key":"very_dry","label":"Very dry","color":"#8c510a","note":"driest 10% of years"},{"key":"dry","label":"Dry","color":"#d8b365","note":"driest 10 to 30% of years"},{"key":"normal","label":"Near normal","color":"#f3efe4","note":"middle 40% of years"},{"key":"wet","label":"Wet","color":"#5ab4ac","note":"wettest 10 to 30% of years"},{"key":"very_wet","label":"Very wet","color":"#01665e","note":"wettest 10% of years"},{"key":"not_rated","label":"Not rated","note":"no usable land cell","color":"#bdbdbd"}],"validation":"soil","rank":{"high":"Wettest compared with normal","low":"Driest compared with normal","note":"Ranked by soil moisture as a percentage of normal."},"how":"Average soil moisture in the top metre of soil for the month, compared with the same month in every year since 2015. A parish is \"very dry\" when the month is among the driest 10% of those years and \"dry\" when it is among the driest 30%. Wet works the other way.","limits":["The values come from NASA SMAP Level 4, which feeds satellite readings into a land model. They are modelled estimates of moisture at depth, not direct measurements.","The record starts in 2015, so normal is based on 11 earlier years and the rankings are coarse.","The grid is about 9 km. It works for parish averages and not for single fields, and small parishes rest on one or two cells.","Satellite readings are weak over forest, marsh and open water, so coastal values lean more on the model.","Each month uses up to 6 sample days at one time of day, not every hour.","The table under the map compares SMAP with the station soil sensors. Those only go back 6 to 21 months, so the check is limited."],"credits":[{"text":"Soil moisture: NASA SMAP L4 (SPL4SMGP), NSIDC DAAC","url":"https://nsidc.